WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.640
A Marvel juggernaut that flamed&nbsp;
out after only one sequel. An edgy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.840
stylized franchise that absolutely bombed on&nbsp;
its second outing. Blade Runner? More like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.920
Blade done-er. These 11 franchises could have been&nbsp;
huge. Instead, they flopped after just one sequel.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.120
In 2002, "Scooby-Doo" proved to be a&nbsp;
box office hit. And how could it not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.680
be? A live-action adaptation of one of&nbsp;
the most popular cartoons in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.280
"Scooby-Doo" was a wacky romp that could appeal&nbsp;
to youngsters and nostalgic adults alike. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.560
live-action adaptations of cartoons tend to&nbsp;
be projects that only work financially once:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.400
the novelty has worn off for most audiences after&nbsp;
a single installment. "The Smurfs 2," "Garfield:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.720
A Tail of Two Kitties," "Peter Rabbit&nbsp;
2…" the list of examples go on and on.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.480
"Scooby-Doo" also suffered from this phenomenon,&nbsp;
as its sequel, "Scooby-Doo 2: Monsters Unleashed,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:58.560
was a box office disappointment. The film's&nbsp;
$181 million worldwide gross was roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.320
35% worse than the global box office haul of&nbsp;
its predecessor. "Monsters Unleashed" may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.440
delivered more familiar "Scooby-Doo" foes from&nbsp;
the original cartoon, but this new installment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.240
suffered from mixed reviews along with a less&nbsp;
hospitable March release date compared to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.520
first film's June launchpad. With these financial&nbsp;
shortcomings, another theatrical live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.880
"Scooby-Doo," penned by James Gunn and meant&nbsp;
to take place in Scotland, was given the ax.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.040
"We're gonna die!"
"Think positive!"&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.240
"We're gonna die quickly!"

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.760
Lara Croft as a video game character&nbsp;
has been plundering ruins for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.680
which has turned this fictional figure into one of&nbsp;
the more enduring characters of 1990s video games.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.960
On the big screen, though, Lara Croft hasn't been&nbsp;
so lucky, with the original live-action movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.640
adaptation of "Tomb Raider" — starring Angelina&nbsp;
Jolie — sputtering out after just two entries.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.240
It didn't initially seem like the&nbsp;
franchise would be so short-lived:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.760
"Lara Croft: Tomb Raider," made serious bank at&nbsp;
the worldwide box office in the summer of 2001.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.320
Capitalizing on the popularity of Croft&nbsp;
and Jolie's increasing star power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.480
the film came along at the perfect&nbsp;
time in every respect. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.400
it wasn't particularly beloved by audiences or&nbsp;
critics despite its sizable box office returns.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.120
When "Lara Croft: The Cradle of Life"&nbsp;
dropped into cinemas in the summer of 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.920
moviegoers gave the production the cold shoulder.&nbsp;
With the film raking in significantly less cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.760
than its predecessor, it was game over&nbsp;
for the "Lara Croft" movie franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.000
Thanks to this extremely disappointing box&nbsp;
office development, this staple of video game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.880
history wouldn't find its way back to movie&nbsp;
theaters until its reboot 15 years later.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.440
The original "The Exorcist" wasn't just a hit,&nbsp;
it was a pop culture sensation. There was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.000
going back for American horror after the&nbsp;
release of William Friedkin's magnum opus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.040
with every scary movie released in its wake&nbsp;
existing in its shadow. This level of influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.280
meant it was only a matter of time before&nbsp;
the producers of the movie decided to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.080
their hand at continuing the story. Despite&nbsp;
the definitive ending of the original film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.760
"Exorcist II: The Heretic" hit theaters&nbsp;
in 1977, four years after the original.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.000
While "The Exorcist" was greeted with universal&nbsp;
praise, "The Heretic" became one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.360
worst-reviewed horror movies of all time. Original&nbsp;
"Exorcist" director William Friedkin was among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.960
chorus of moviegoers who despised the project,&nbsp;
going so far as to declare that "The Heretic"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.560
was one of the worst films he'd ever watched.&nbsp;
What inspired this deluge of vitriol? Mostly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.000
confounding plot and moments that elicited giggles&nbsp;
rather than screams. With this kind of response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.600
the prospect of an ongoing "Exorcist" franchise&nbsp;
suddenly seemed a lot less probable than before.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.800
"The Exorcist" only wound up being&nbsp;
resurrected in 1990 when William Peter Blatty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.280
author of the novel the original "Exorcist"&nbsp;
was based on, decided to write and direct a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.840
third installment himself. "The Exorcist&nbsp;
III" is much more well-regarded than "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.840
Heretic" — probably because its creator actually&nbsp;
had an understanding of the source material.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.520
Vin Diesel is best known for being&nbsp;
fast and furious on the silver screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.160
but Dominic Toretto isn't his only character&nbsp;
that loves a good burst of adrenaline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:15.120
In 2002's "xXx," Diesel plays Xander Cage, a super&nbsp;
spy who uses extreme sports to save the world.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.040
"You've just entered… the Xander zone."

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.240
"xXx" was a solid box office performer and&nbsp;
was enough of a moneymaker to ensure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.920
it would get a couple of sequels. If&nbsp;
James Bond could keep going and going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.360
Xander Cage could surely&nbsp;
sustain a few more installments.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.640
A sequel did emerge with the 2005 feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.960
"xXx: State of the Union," but the production&nbsp;
had a massive issue with Diesel — or rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.760
his absence. To the producers' dismay,&nbsp;
Diesel chose not to return for the sequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.320
and had to be replaced by Ice Cube. While the&nbsp;
rapper-turned-actor is certainly no slouch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.640
the original idea was to create an iconic&nbsp;
character that people could follow for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.040
endless adventures — not somebody who would&nbsp;
get swapped out after a single installment.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.680
Unsurprisingly, "State of the Union" crumbled&nbsp;
at the box office and destroyed any hopes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.920
a sprawling franchise. While the "Fast and&nbsp;
Furious" movies have managed to deliver new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.040
adventures on a regular basis, it would take more&nbsp;
than a decade for another "xXx" installment, "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.320
Return of Xander Cage," to hit theaters, with —&nbsp;
you guessed it — Diesel returning as Xander Cage.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.000
The original "Blade Runner" wasn't exactly&nbsp;
a box office smash, but it proved to be so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.040
enormously influential that a sequel was an&nbsp;
inevitability. After years of development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:33.360
"Blade Runner 2049" hit theaters in October&nbsp;
2017, courtesy of director Denis Villeneuve.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.080
Before the sequel's release, Villeneuve and&nbsp;
producer Ridley Scott openly discussed their hopes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.360
and story ideas for a potential "Blade Runner 3."&nbsp;
Sadly, those hopes were immediately threatened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.040
by the box office earnings. Despite lots of&nbsp;
pre-release hype, "2049" underperformed on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.080
opening weekend. What once looked like a surefire&nbsp;
way to re-energize the "Blade Runner" universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.040
suddenly wasn't looking so promising. This&nbsp;
critical problem didn't get rectified in the weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.640
afterward either, as "2049" ended up losing money&nbsp;
once its worldwide box office run was finished.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:08.800
With this development, the idea of doing&nbsp;
further "Blade Runner" movies was shelved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.840
with Villeneuve moving his sci-fi storytelling&nbsp;
abilities into the world of "Dune" instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:17.200
Despite both entries in the&nbsp;
franchise scoring critical acclaim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:21.840
box office woes have so far kept further "Blade&nbsp;
Runner" movies from gracing the silver screen.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.680
Box office expectations often miss severely&nbsp;
when it comes to comedy sequels. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.160
projects like "City Slickers" and "Horrible&nbsp;
Bosses" made big bucks at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.280
their respective follow-ups just failed to click&nbsp;
with moviegoers. Still, the allure of delivering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.560
the rare "The Hangover: Part II" or "22 Jump&nbsp;
Street" at the box office means that studios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.200
still try their luck at launching hit comedy&nbsp;
franchises. This includes a follow-up to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:52.480
2014 sleeper hit "Neighbors," which ended up&nbsp;
becoming one of Seth Rogen's biggest movies.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.120
The same could not be said for "Neighbors 2:&nbsp;
Sorority Rising," a follow-up that flipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.440
the script by having Rogen and Rose&nbsp;
Byrne team up with their former enemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.080
Zac Efron, to take down a sorority house.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.400
"That's right: I'm switching sides."

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.360
The result was a project that just didn't&nbsp;
connect with moviegoers, partially because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:16.000
the premise didn't seem all that different from&nbsp;
the original. Although technically profitable by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:22.720
making $108 million worldwide on a $35 million&nbsp;
budget, "Neighbors 2" was a massive step down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:27.600
from the original "Neighbors" film. Any potential&nbsp;
for "Neighbors" taking a cue from "The Hangover"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.720
and going on for an entire trilogy was stopped&nbsp;
cold with "Sorority Rising," which turned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.920
to be both a franchise killer and yet another&nbsp;
example of an underperforming comedy sequel.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.640
At the start of the 2010s, director&nbsp;
Roland Emmerich announced that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:46.640
were finally plans to make a sequel&nbsp;
to "Independence Day." Not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.720
but the filmmaker openly discussed his&nbsp;
ambitions to make multiple follow-ups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.640
to his 1996 sci-fi disaster movie. Even though&nbsp;
only a single sequel was eventually greenlit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.960
Emmerich was eager to discuss his detailed&nbsp;
ambitions for the future of the series.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.520
Ultimately, however, Emmerich would not get a&nbsp;
chance to realize the entirety of his vision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.080
The original "Independence Day" was one of&nbsp;
the biggest box office hits of the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.000
but its sequel was one of the&nbsp;
biggest disappointments of 2016.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.920
The project's financial troubles could&nbsp;
be chalked up to all sorts of factors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.480
including intense competition from other&nbsp;
blockbusters that summer along with relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.480
lousy marketing. Whatever the reasons for&nbsp;
"Resurgence" failing to meet box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.120
expectations were, it was quickly made clear that&nbsp;
there would not be a third "Independence Day."

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:37.440
Nobody involved with the original "Speed"&nbsp;
imagined the movie sustaining a series of sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:42.960
especially not director Jan de Bont. Alas,&nbsp;
the film's massive box office intake meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.360
that de Bont was contractually obligated to&nbsp;
direct a second installment— but this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.480
it would be without Keanu Reeves.&nbsp;
The loss of their leading man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.080
should've been the first warning sign&nbsp;
for the producers at 20th Century Fox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.080
but that didn't stop them from pouring&nbsp;
over $110 million into the budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.400
It certainly didn't help that the intense and&nbsp;
claustrophobic setting of a bus from the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.680
"Speed" was traded out for a massive boat — an&nbsp;
idea that de Bont claimed came to him in a dream.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:11.520
Of course, "Speed 2: Cruise Control"&nbsp;
ended up being more of a nightmare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.800
June 1997 was an exceptionally crowded time for&nbsp;
blockbusters, meaning the film had to compete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.440
with the likes of "Batman &amp; Robin," "Con&nbsp;
Air," and "The Lost World: Jurassic Park."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.000
It didn't stand a chance, and "Cruise Control"&nbsp;
quickly became nothing more than a punchline.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.840
It's unknown if there were any initial&nbsp;
plans to develop a "Speed 3," but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.600
failure of this doomed follow-up&nbsp;
ensured that studio executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.040
wouldn't try to kick the "Speed"&nbsp;
franchise into high gear again.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.880
The enduring popularity of Lee Child's Jack&nbsp;
Reacher books meant that it was only a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:47.600
of time before Reacher would get to star in&nbsp;
a theatrical film. This opportunity finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:53.360
emerged in December 2012 with "Jack Reacher,"&nbsp;
which functioned as yet another star vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.800
for Tom Cruise. The production wasn't as big of a&nbsp;
hit as the novels, but "Jack Reacher" still proved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.760
reasonably profitable on its more restrained&nbsp;
budget. Paramount Pictures, which wasn't exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:08.240
bursting with franchises in the mid-2010s,&nbsp;
was willing to gamble on "Jack Reacher."

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:14.240
A sequel, "Jack Reacher: Never Go Back," wouldn't&nbsp;
emerge until October 2016 — a whopping four years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.800
after its predecessor debuted in theaters.&nbsp;
The original "Jack Reacher" was well-liked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:24.240
but was it beloved enough to maintain&nbsp;
audience interest for so long? No, not really.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.960
"You're right. The numbers don't add up."

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:32.480
"Never Go Back" ended up flopping, killing&nbsp;
off "Jack Reacher" as a viable theatrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.080
franchise in the process. When the&nbsp;
character finally did re-appear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:40.240
it was in an Amazon TV series starring&nbsp;
Alan Ritchson in the title role.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.960
Certain Marvel superheroes have&nbsp;
no shortage of big-screen hits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:50.000
Iron Man and Captain America have both received&nbsp;
plenty of solo outings and crossover appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.360
while "Spider-Man" seems destined to be rebooted&nbsp;
and reimagined over and over again. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:59.040
there are plenty of other figures from&nbsp;
this comic book company's vast library of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.200
characters that have been less successful&nbsp;
at maintaining longevity in theaters.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.560
Case in point: Ghost Rider, a darker, more edgy&nbsp;
superhero that initially seemed to be poised for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:13.680
franchise success when he made his big-screen&nbsp;
debut in 2007. The first "Ghost Rider" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.000
which starred Nicolas Cage in the title&nbsp;
role, was a solid box office performer.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:21.520
"Ghost Rider" yielded a sequel, "Ghost&nbsp;
Rider: Spirit of Vengeance," but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.160
were a few problems that ended up causing the&nbsp;
franchise to crash and burn. Most glaringly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.600
there was a five-year gap between the&nbsp;
two "Ghost Rider" movies — a stretch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:32.720
time during which the first two&nbsp;
"Iron Man" films were released.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.520
At the end of the day, this new "Ghost Rider"&nbsp;
simply didn't offer anything new to audiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:42.240
An R rating might have saved the series, but&nbsp;
executives decided to play it safe instead.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:45.360
While there were originally plans&nbsp;
for a third "Ghost Rider," the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:48.960
dismal box office intake of "Spirit&nbsp;
of Vengeance" slammed the brakes on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:52.960
those prospects. The character has yet&nbsp;
to surface in the MCU, but a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:57.200
version of the character made an appearance on&nbsp;
Marvel's "Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D." TV series.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.520
When "Sin City" first hit theaters in April 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:06.320
it was considered new and fresh. Mainstream&nbsp;
American cinema wasn't exactly yearning for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:11.920
more noir tributes, but Robert Rodriguez's&nbsp;
heavily stylized world made the film stand out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.400
from the rest. With several other "Sin City"&nbsp;
stories from the comics yet to be adapted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:21.440
it seemed like only a matter of time before&nbsp;
"Sin City" became an ongoing franchise. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:27.120
it took nearly a decade before "Sin City: A Dame&nbsp;
to Kill For" finally ended up in movie theaters.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:32.640
By the time "A Dame to Kill For" rolled around in&nbsp;
August 2014, the cinematic landscape had changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:38.000
dramatically. The visual look of "Sin City" had&nbsp;
already been copied to death by other filmmakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:42.640
and audiences had simply moved on —&nbsp;
resulting in a disastrous box office haul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:46.080
Any plans for additional "Sin City"&nbsp;
movies were immediately shelved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:50.640
unlikely ever to see the light of day. What&nbsp;
had once seemed like a foolproof franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.720
quickly turned into two movies&nbsp;
with vastly differing receptions.

